  "summary": "A formal kimono style with long, flowing sleeves worn by unmarried women.",
  "description": "Characterized by its long sleeves that swing dramatically with movement, furisode features elaborate patterns often covering the entire garment. This design element emphasizes youth and availability for marriage in traditional Japanese society.",
  "culturalContext": "The furisode — kimono with 80-114cm swinging sleeves — is unmarried women's ceremonial wear: Coming-of-Age Day (Seijin no Hi) furisode in saturated yuzen dyeing, wedding-guest golds and florals, obi knots (fukura-suzume) as architecture. Rental industries now democratize it; the furisode's sleeve-waving dance gestures (in kabuki and kyōgen) tie fashion to performance, and its image is Japan's strongest \"special occasion\" visual code.",
